course: dictionary
lesson: 4533
title: Heap, a pile and a lot
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A heap is a big, untidy pile of things on top of each other. «She lands on a heap of sticks and dry leaves» tells you the sticks and leaves are piled up loosely, not neatly stacked. The words around heap give the clue: sticks and leaves are messy things, so the heap is messy too.
explain: Heap can also mean a lot of something. At the street party, «a heap of kids» means lots of children, not a pile of children. Read the words next to heap and ask: does this sound like a pile of things, or a big amount? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check.
explain: Heap is also a doing word. To heap is to pile things up, or to fill something right up to the top, like a heaped spoon. «He collapses in a heap in the road» shows a person falling into a crumpled, untidy shape. So heap can be a thing you can see, or something you do.
fact: Heap is a noun (a heap, heaps) and a verb (to heap, heaped, heaping).
fact: «A heap» means a big untidy pile, like a heap of laundry or a compost heap.
fact: «Heaps of» means a lot of something. It is informal, and very common in Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «A heaped teaspoon» is a spoon filled so the sugar or flour piles up above the top edge.
fact: «Heaped» is the past form of the verb heap, so «She heaped the leaves» means she piled them up.
exercise: «She landed on a heap of sticks and dry leaves and was not a bit hurt.» What is a heap here? | Small and neat, like a box | *A big untidy pile | A loud noise | A kind of tree
exercise: Mum says, «Heaps of kids came to the street party.» What does heaps of mean? | Very cold | A kind of game | *A lot of | Only a few
exercise: Ella fills her spoon so the sugar piles up over the edge. Which word means this? | To tidy | *To heap | To wash | To whisper
test: «He collapses in a heap in the road.» What does «in a heap» suggest about how he fell? | Very slowly | On purpose, for a rest | *In a crumpled, messy shape | Neatly and calmly
test: Sam says, «This game is heaps better than last week.» What does heaps better mean? | Heaped with food | *Much better | About the same | Much worse
exam: Which sentence uses heap as a noun, meaning a pile? | The kids heap the leaves | *A heap of leaves sat by the gate | She will heap the rice on the plate | He is heaping up the sand
